We sought to characterize further the protonic conductivity in our films by interrogating them with EIS as a function of temperature. We therefore formulated Nyquist plots for reflectin devices contacted with blocking gold electrodes on glass over a temperature range from \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 4); reflectin is expected to be stable across this range because it maintains its functionality even after processing at temperatures as high as \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (ref. 47). By fitting the impedance data with the equivalent circuit depicted in Fig. 1, we determined the conductivity of our reflectin films at different temperatures (Fig. 4). At a RH of \(90\%\) and a temperature of \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), we measured a peak conductivity of \(2.6\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{S\,cm}^{-1}\) and an average conductivity of \(1.2(\pm1)\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{S\,cm}^{-1}\) across a set of three independent films (Fig. 4). These values compare favourably to those found for other proton-conducting materials\(^{11-17}\) and are among the best reported for any bulk solid-state material from a naturally occurring protein\(^{21-26}\).
We next constructed Arrhenius-type conductivity plots from our temperature-dependent measurements, which allowed us to determine the activation energy \((E_{\mathrm{a}})\) of proton conduction for reflectin (Fig. 4)\(^{12,53}\). This activation energy corresponds to the cost of dissociating and/or transporting protons through the hydrogen-bonded water networks that presumably permeate our protein films\(^{10,20}\). The average value of \(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.22(\pm0.05)\) eV calculated from a linear fit of our measurements was characteristic of a Grotthuss-type conduction mechanism (Fig. 2)\(^{10,19,20}\), similar activation energies have been found for proton conduction in gramicidin channels
